\section{Head models and the MRI check}\label{sec:S4}

\subsection{The adult and the three classes of smaller head}\label{sec:S4.1}

The adult is the MNE sample subject \citep{gramfort2013}, with FreeSurfer surfaces \citep{fischl2012}, a three-layer boundary-element model (BEM) on the MRI scalp and << F.g3b_adult_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm² of usable cortex. The smaller heads belong to three classes, which are not independent subjects (main text, Section 2.2). The << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults keep the adult's vertices, so $\Delta$ is taken vertex by vertex: the school-age scaled adult is << F.g3b_cfg_school_scale >> times the adult, and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_years >>-year scaled adult has the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. The principal smaller heads are these << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates. The << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template, which the MRI check classed as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >> (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}), and the << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children, whose near-scalp anatomy could not be verified, are reported separately in Section~\ref{sec:S10}.

The << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates \citep{oreilly2021}, averages of many MRIs of the Neurodevelopmental MRI Database \citep{richards2016}, have smooth cortices. Their usable cortex at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, << F.g3b_infant2yr_cortex_area_cm2 >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_cortex_area_cm2 >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm² (<< F.g3b_templates_cortex_area_pct_of_adult_range >> of the adult's), lowers their total background power and their patch cancellation. They were used at native size with their own three-layer BEMs, oct-6 source spaces, atlas labels and fiducials.

The << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years; subjects << F.rev_qc_child_a_subject >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_subject >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_subject >>) come from the de-identified OpenNeuro dataset ds005234, released under a CC0 license \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}, whose authors processed their MRIs. We used their white and sphere surfaces, atlas annotations and dense MRI scalp as distributed. In the distributed file tree, each subject's watershed BEM surfaces are stored in the preceding subject's folder. We matched them to each child by the volume information they share with the child's own surfaces, and the storage objects behind them carry that child's subject name.

These surfaces put the inner skull implausibly close to the scalp (median << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below it over the upper head), with an outer skull crossing it, so we modeled the children's skull. The inner skull was moved inward to << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >> mm below the scalp wherever it lay shallower, with no vertex within << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >> mm of a white-surface vertex. Between vertices it comes within << F.g3b_children_white_to_inner_skull_closest_range >> mm of the white surface, and no white vertex lies outside it. The outer skull lies about halfway to the scalp, and the modeled inner skull a median << F.g3b_children_modelled_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >> mm below the scalp over the upper head. On the adult, whose segmented skull is known, the same procedure applied to a degraded inner skull changed the primary ratio by at most << F.g3b_bemval_max_ratio_change >> over the modeled skull depths. In every head, the forward model's outer surface is the watershed skin placed on the MRI scalp.

The dataset has no fiducials, which define the head frame and hence the centered placement, the origin of every scaled helmet and the OPM coverage region. We transferred them from the adult by a similarity fit of the cortices and moved them to the nearest scalp vertex. On the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, this reproduced their own fiducials with errors of << F.g3b_principal_templates_fid_transfer_range >> mm and their head frames with rotations of << F.g3b_principal_templates_fid_transfer_rotation_range >>°. On the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template, the errors were << F.g3b_provisional_fid_transfer_range >> mm and the rotation << F.g3b_infant18mo_fid_transfer_rotation >>°; the largest error was at the nasion, the fiducial farthest from the cortex. Such total rotations are of the order of the placement family's (pitch ±<< F.g3b_cfg_pitch >>°, yaw ±<< F.g3b_cfg_yaw >>°, roll ±<< F.g3b_cfg_roll >>°; Section~\ref{sec:S5.1}), which indicates rather than bounds their effect. The transferred fiducials were not checked on the children's own MRIs. Fiducials from Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) coordinates were not used: on the adult, they land << F.g3b_adult_mni_fid_range >> mm from the digitized ones.

Every head uses the adult's conductivities (<< F.meth_conductivity >> S/m). A single-compartment BEM (<< F.meth_bem1_conductivity >> S/m inside the inner skull) has no skull or scalp conductivity layer, but its one boundary is the inner skull of the three-layer model, the modeled one in the children. With it, the difference of medians from the adult in the principal smaller heads was << F.g3b_principal_bem1_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB, against << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB with three layers. The change was << F.meth_principal_bem1_change_db_range >> dB, and << F.meth_provisional_bem1_change_db_range >> dB in the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}. This check tests the conductivity layers outside the inner skull, not the location of the inner skull, which the two models share. As distributed, the << F.meth_infant12mo_age_months >>-month template's skull is << F.meth_infant12mo_skull_min_mm >> mm thick at its thinnest (<< F.meth_infant12mo_thin_vertices >> of << F.meth_infant12mo_inner_skull_vertices >> inner-skull vertices within << F.meth_infant12mo_thin_within_mm >> mm of the outer skull). With the single-compartment BEM, its $D$ was << F.meth_infant12mo_d_bem1_db >> dB (three layers: << F.meth_infant12mo_d_db >> dB) and its difference from the adult << F.meth_infant12mo_d_minus_adult_bem1_db >> dB (<< F.meth_infant12mo_d_minus_adult_db >> dB), so the thin skull does not carry its result.

\subsection{Regions}\label{sec:S4.2}

Regions are the lobes of the Desikan--Killiany atlas \citep{desikan2006}. Frontal: superior, rostral middle and caudal middle frontal, pars opercularis, triangularis and orbitalis, lateral and medial orbitofrontal, precentral, paracentral and frontal pole. Parietal: superior and inferior parietal, supramarginal, postcentral and precuneus. Temporal: superior, middle and inferior temporal, banks of the superior temporal sulcus, fusiform, transverse temporal, entorhinal, temporal pole and parahippocampal. Occipital: lateral occipital, lingual, cuneus and pericalcarine. Cingulate: rostral and caudal anterior, posterior and isthmus. The sixth region is the insula. The medial wall belongs to no region, and both hemispheres are pooled.

We also report parcels emphasized in the epilepsy literature, precentral, superior temporal (``lateral temporal'' in main-text Fig.~7) and parahippocampal, and a group of the parahippocampal and entorhinal parcels, which is what ``mesial temporal'' always means here. With all sources on the cortical surface and no hippocampus or amygdala, this cortex (<< F.reg_mesial_parcels_depth_range >> mm below the adult's scalp, median over targets) is the nearest proxy for mesial temporal generators. The regions were chosen from the literature, and rankings among them are exploratory.

\subsection{The MRI quality check of the school-aged children and the infant templates}\label{sec:S4.3}

The children's white surfaces come within << F.g3b_children_white_to_scalp_closest_range >> mm of the scalp used for sensor placement and source depths. Child B has << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> targets shallower than << F.g3b_shallow_target_limit_mm >> mm (adult: none). Its head is the size of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template's (head circumference << F.g3b_child_b_ofc_cm >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_ofc_cm >> cm; cap volume << F.g3b_child_b_cap_volume_cm3 >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_cap_volume_cm3 >> cm³), but its cortex is much larger (<< F.g3b_child_b_cortex_area_cm2 >> against << F.g3b_infant2yr_cortex_area_cm2 >> cm²). Both suggested a scalp lying inside the true scalp or misregistered relative to the white surface. We therefore checked the children's surfaces, exactly as the forward models use them, against their T1 images, with the adult and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template as references. We then checked the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates.

The T1 intensity was sampled along the outward normal of every dense-scalp vertex, from << F.rev_qc_profile_range_mm >> mm in << F.rev_qc_profile_step_mm >>-mm steps. The MRI head boundary is where this profile falls through the volume's air/tissue threshold (Otsu's method; primary), with the half-maximum edge and the steepest gradient as secondary definitions. Its signed offset from the scalp used (positive outward) was summarized over the cap, the head above the fiducial plane, by median, quartiles and 5th to 95th percentiles, and decomposed into a uniform offset, a translation and a rotation. Frames were checked by comparing each surface's volume geometry with the T1 header. White-surface registration was checked by the white/gray contrast across the surface, searching coarsely then finely for the rigid shift that maximizes it and reporting that shift's contrast gain. Head circumference and the targets below << F.rev_qc_near_mm >> mm were measured against both the boundary and the scalp used.

A head is misregistered if a surface's header differs from the T1 by more than the header tolerance, or if the white surface's realigning shift, edge translation or rotation displacement exceeds << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >> mm. It is inside or outside if the cap median offset exceeds, or falls short of, the adult's by more than << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >> mm (one voxel) at both the Otsu and the half-maximum edge, and usable otherwise. On the adult, a declared inward scalp displacement of << F.rev_qc_test_inward_declared_mm >> mm was recovered as << F.rev_qc_test_inward_recovered_mm >> mm, and a << F.rev_qc_test_translation_declared_mm >>-mm translation as << F.rev_qc_test_translation_recovered_mm >> mm (<< F.rev_qc_test_translation_explained_pct >> of the offset pattern explained by a translation). Undoing a << F.rev_qc_test_white_shift_declared_mm >>-mm shift of its white surface raised the contrast by << F.rev_qc_test_white_contrast_gain_pct >>, so a misregistered white surface would be found.

Every surface's header matched its T1 (corner displacements at most << F.rev_qc_children_frame_max_disp_mm >> mm in the children; Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}). The children's white surfaces were registered: best realigning shift << F.rev_qc_children_white_best_shift_max_mm >> mm, edge translation at most << F.rev_qc_children_white_translation_max_mm >> mm, rotation displacement at most << F.rev_qc_children_white_rotation_disp_max_mm >> mm, and <% if F.rev_qc_child_a_white_contrast_gain_pct == F.rev_qc_child_b_white_contrast_gain_pct and F.rev_qc_child_b_white_contrast_gain_pct == F.rev_qc_child_c_white_contrast_gain_pct %>a contrast gain of << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_contrast_gain_pct >> in each child<% else %>contrast gains of << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_contrast_gain_pct >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_contrast_gain_pct >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_contrast_gain_pct >> (children A, B and C)<% endif %>. The children's scalp used lay a median << F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_otsu_mm_range >> mm inside the MRI head boundary at the Otsu edge (<< F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_halfmax_mm_range >> mm at the half-maximum edge), against << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >> and << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_halfmax_mm >> mm in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}C). A FreeSurfer scalp thus lies just inside the T1 edge in every individual head.

The << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template's scalp lay << F.wr_qc_infant2yr_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> mm outside its boundary (verdict: << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_verdict >>), consistent with an average template whose scalp is smoothed outward. The << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates' scalps lay << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> and << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_offset_outside_otsu_mm >> mm outside (<< F.wr_qc_infant18mo_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> and << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> mm at the half-maximum edge, against << F.wr_qc_infant2yr_offset_outside_halfmax_mm >> mm at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >> months). The verdict was << F.wr_qc_infant12mo_verdict >> at << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months and << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >> at << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> months. The latter's white surface fitted the T1 contrast best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift, gaining << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_contrast_gain_pct >> (other templates: << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >> mm), with an edge translation of << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_edge_translation_mm >> mm, both beyond the << F.rev_qc_tolerance_mm >>-mm tolerance. This whole-surface misfit is not visible in single sections; its white vertex closest to the scalp used lies << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm below it (sections not shown). Whether it reflects a displaced surface or the weaker contrast of an average image was not settled. No template surface was repaired, and the offset was not propagated to the template's results. For this reason, the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template is reported with the children in Section~\ref{sec:S10}.

The head circumference on the MRI boundary minus that on the scalp used was << F.rev_qc_children_ofc_mri_minus_surface_mm_range >> mm in the children (<< F.rev_qc_children_ofc_mri_cm_range >> against << F.rev_qc_children_ofc_surface_cm_range >> cm) and << F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_mri_minus_surface_mm >> mm in the adult (<< F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_mri_cm >> against << F.rev_qc_adult_ofc_surface_cm >> cm). Measured from the boundary, the median target depth changed by << F.rev_qc_children_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the children, << F.rev_qc_adult_targets_depth_change_p50_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.wr_qc_templates_targets_depth_change_p50_mm_range >> mm in the templates, whose scalps lie outside their boundaries. The children's medians thus shifted as the adult's did; their shallowest targets did not.

The closest white-surface vertex lay << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_scalp_min_mm >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_scalp_min_mm >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm below the scalp used in children A, B and C (<< F.rev_qc_children_white_mri_min_mm_range >> mm below the MRI boundary). It lay << F.rev_qc_adult_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm below it in the adult and << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_white_scalp_min_mm >> mm in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template (Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}A). The 5th percentile of white-surface depth below the scalp used was << F.rev_qc_children_white_scalp_p5_mm_range >> mm in the children, << F.rev_qc_adult_white_scalp_p5_mm >> mm in the adult and << F.rev_qc_infant2yr_white_scalp_p5_mm >> mm in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. Within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the scalp used lay << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_scalp_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> cm² of the children's white surface, and within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the MRI boundary << F.rev_qc_child_a_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_white_mri_lt8mm_area_cm2 >> cm² (adult: none; Fig.~\ref{fig:S6}B). In no child does the T1 head mask reach a volume face other than the inferior one, which it reaches at the neck in children B and C, as in the adult. The field of view therefore does not cut the head above the neck.

Of the cortical targets of children A, B and C, << F.rev_qc_child_a_targets_lt8mm_n >>, << F.rev_qc_child_b_targets_lt8mm_n >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_targets_lt8mm_n >> lay below << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm and << F.rev_qc_child_a_targets_lt10mm_n >>, << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> and << F.rev_qc_child_c_targets_lt10mm_n >> below << F.wr_qc_near_hi_mm >> mm (adult: << F.rev_qc_adult_targets_lt10mm_n >>; full-precision depths). Measured to the MRI boundary, child B's targets less than << F.wr_qc_near_hi_mm >> mm deep fell from << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> to << F.wr_qc_child_b_targets_lt10mm_mri_n >>. <% if F.rev_qc_n_children_usable == F.g3b_n_children_heads %>All << F.g3b_n_children_heads|words >> children were classed << F.rev_qc_child_a_verdict >>, and no surface was repaired.<% else %>The verdicts were child A << F.rev_qc_child_a_verdict >>, child B << F.rev_qc_child_b_verdict >> and child C << F.rev_qc_child_c_verdict >> (<< F.rev_qc_n_children_usable >> of << F.g3b_n_children_heads >> usable), and no surface was repaired.<% endif %>

The children's shallow cortex, and child B's small head around a near-adult cortex, are therefore not artifacts of scalp placement or registration, judged against the adult rather than a pediatric norm. The data leave open a head boundary that the T1 images themselves place too deep. Such a boundary would bring the FreeSurfer scalp, which follows the image's edge, toward the cortex together with the watershed surfaces (Section~\ref{sec:S4.1}). A check against the same images cannot detect it, but an independent measurement of scalp and skull over the upper head, from another sequence or modality, would. Nor can the check settle whether the white surface is anatomically correct within a few millimeters of the scalp, where the modeled scalp-and-skull layer is thinner than the adult's. It also cannot settle whether three children of one dataset represent their age: the verdicts test registration and scalp position, not tissue thicknesses or head circumference against norms for age.

The source rule, no source within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >> mm of the inner skull, left out << F.wr_excl_child_a_pct >>, << F.wr_excl_child_b_pct >> and << F.wr_excl_child_c_pct >> of the white-surface area of children A, B and C. It left out << F.wr_excl_adult_pct >> in the adult, << F.wr_excl_scaled_pct_range >> in the scaled adults and << F.wr_excl_templates_pct_range >> in the templates. The adult's share is the << F.rev_ns_near_area_pct >> of Section~\ref{sec:S3.6} plus the little cortex outside the inner skull (<< F.wr_excl_adult_lt10mm_cm2 >> cm² of it within << F.wr_qc_near_hi_mm >> mm of the scalp). In the children it includes << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the cortex within << F.wr_qc_near_lo_mm >> mm of the scalp; of child B's << F.wr_excl_child_b_lt8mm_n >> white-surface vertices there, << F.wr_excl_child_b_lt8mm_usable_n >> pass the rule. The children's results therefore do not describe a near-scalp cortex. With the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template's, they are reported in Section~\ref{sec:S10} and enter none of the principal results.

